Hall of the Three Saints (三聖殿)

Lian Shan Shuang Lin Monastery
Preliminary observation
The Hall of the Three Saints is named after the Three Saints of the West (西方三聖, Xīfāng Sān Shèng) — the canonical Pure Land iconographic triad of Amitābha Buddha and his two attendant bodhisattvas. The hall is therefore explicitly a Pure Land devotional hall, dedicated to the iconographic and ritual programme of Pure Land Buddhist practice. The hall name does not name a function (as "Ritual Hall" would have); it names the deities. The monastery's institutional self-understanding of this space is iconographic-devotional rather than functional.
This is consistent with the iconographic configuration documented in the photograph. The Western Triad in standing form, on lotus pedestals, with Amitābha holding a lotus in his hands (the canonical attribute for receiving souls into the Pure Land), encoded the soteriological programme of Pure Land Buddhism: rebirth in the Western Pure Land at the moment of death, mediated by Avalokiteśvara's compassion and Mahāsthāmaprāpta's wisdom.
The hall's functional uses — Pure Land devotional chanting (nianfo 念佛), memorial services, funerary rites, daily monastic chanting — are activities that the iconographic programme supports rather than the identity that defines the hall. The hall is for these activities because of the Three Saints it houses, not the other way around.

Step 1 — ICON descriptions
ICON₁ — Amitābha Buddha (阿彌陀佛) (Central, rear)
POSITION    : central, axial, elevated
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, cosmological
Standing Buddha form with monastic robes and ushnisha, holding a lotus in his hands (the canonical attribute for receiving souls into the Pure Land), framed by an elaborate flame-leaf mandorla. His soteriological function is specifically Pure Land in orientation — he receives the souls of the deceased into the Western Pure Land, where they continue their progress toward Buddhahood under conditions ideal for spiritual cultivation.

ICON₂ — Avalokiteśvara (觀世音菩薩) (Right of Amitābha, viewer's left or right depending on canonical arrangement)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral, elevated
SCALE       : equal (relative to Mahāsthāmaprāpta),
              subordinate (relative to Amitābha)
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, intercessory, devotional
ICON₃ — Mahāsthāmaprāpta (大勢至菩薩) (Left of Amitābha)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral, elevated
SCALE       : equal (relative to Avalokiteśvara),
              subordinate (relative to Amitābha)
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional
The two flanking bodhisattvas wear elaborate crowns and hold lotus stems. Avalokiteśvara represents the karuṇā (compassion) dimension of Pure Land soteriology — guiding souls toward Amitābha through compassionate response. Mahāsthāmaprāpta represents the prajñā (wisdom) dimension — illuminating the path toward rebirth through the power of awakened insight. Together with Amitābha they constitute the canonical Western Triad: the Buddha at the centre, compassion and wisdom as his two inseparable attendants.

ICON₄ and ICON₅ — Two seated attendant figures (Middle register, between and below the main triad)
POSITION    : peripheral, recessed, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : inward-facing or front-facing
              (provisional pending closer observation)
FUNCTION    : pedagogical, devotional
              (provisional)
These smaller golden seated figures with halos appear positioned as attendants to the central programme. Their identification requires closer fieldwork observation — possible candidates include Ānanda and Mahākāśyapa (the canonical disciples), seated bodhisattva attendants, or smaller emanations of the principal figures. The analysis treats them as paired pedagogical/devotional attendants pending confirmation.

ICON₆ — (Sangharama Deva / Guan Gong)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : outward-facing
FUNCTION    : protective, apotropaic, administrative
ICON₇ — (Skanda / Wei Tuo)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : outward-facing
FUNCTION    : protective, apotropaic
The two armored figures at the front of the altar platform appear to be the canonical paired Buddhist monastery guardians — Sangharama Deva (the bearded, more wrathful figure on the viewer's left) and Skanda (the youthful golden armored figure on the viewer's right). Their presence flanking the Western Triad is iconographically standard for ritual halls: the soteriological programme at the centre is protected by the institutional and dharma-protective figures at the periphery.

ICON₈ — Front altar Avalokiteśvara (seated)
POSITION    : central, axial, recessed (front offering altar)
SCALE       : singular (front altar context)
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : devotional, intercessory
The small seated golden Avalokiteśvara on the front offering altar, framed by the monastery's name embroidered on the yellow cloth panel, occupies a distinct iconographic position from the Avalokiteśvara within the Western Triad. She is the devotional point of immediate ritual address — the figure to whom offerings on the front altar are presented — while the Avalokiteśvara within the triad participates in the larger soteriological programme presided over by Amitābha.

