Step 3 — The Western Triad as soteriological cosmogram
The Western Triad is one of the two canonical triadic Buddha programmes in Chinese Buddhist iconography, the other being the Three Buddhas of the Mahavira Hall. The two encode different soteriological logics. The Three Buddhas in the Mahavira Hall — Śākyamuni flanked by Amitābha and Bhaiṣajyaguru — represent the cosmological structure of Buddhahood across the three realms (present world, Western Pure Land, Eastern Lapis Lazuli realm). The Western Triad represents the soteriological mechanism of Pure Land rebirth specifically: Amitābha receives, Avalokiteśvara guides through compassion, Mahāsthāmaprāpta illuminates through wisdom.
The repetition of Amitābha across both halls — flanking Śākyamuni in the Mahavira Hall, central in the Ritual Hall — is theologically significant. In the Mahavira Hall, Amitābha is one Buddha among three, situated within the cosmological completeness of the Buddhist universe. In the Ritual Hall, he is the central programmatic figure, the soteriological agent of the specific operation the hall is designed to perform: the reception of souls into the Pure Land at the moment of death and through commemorative rites afterward.
